  <p>
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> Fix The Speaker plays low-frequency sound waves that vibrate the phone's speaker
    diaphragm. These physical vibrations produce small, rapid air pulses that can help push trapped water droplets and
    loose dust particles out through the fine mesh of the speaker grille.
  </p>

  <h2>What the tool does: exact frequencies</h2>
  <p>
    All tones are generated directly within your web browser in real time using the client-side HTML5 Web Audio API. No
    audio files are streamed or stored, and no app installation is needed.
  </p>
  <p>The code uses specific acoustic frequencies depending on the selected mode:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Water Eject mode:</strong> Plays a low-frequency tone centered on a <strong>165&nbsp;Hz</strong> square-wave
      core, reinforced with an <strong>82.5&nbsp;Hz</strong> sub-bass layer (one octave lower) and a
      <strong>330&nbsp;Hz</strong> harmonic tone (one octave higher). A low-frequency oscillator (LFO) pulses this sound at
      <strong>3.2&nbsp;Hz</strong> through a <strong>200&nbsp;Hz</strong> low-shelf filter (+8&nbsp;dB bass boost) to
      create repeated bursts of air displacement.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dust Cleaner mode:</strong> Plays dual-band high-frequency sawtooth sweeps spanning <strong>2.5&nbsp;kHz to
      6&nbsp;kHz</strong> (mid-band) and <strong>8&nbsp;kHz to 14.5&nbsp;kHz</strong> (high-band), combined with a
      <strong>3.5&nbsp;kHz</strong> presence boost filter to vibrate the speaker grille mesh and help loosen light dust and
      lint.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stereo Diagnostic mode:</strong> Plays calibrated left-channel, right-channel, and stereo-centered tones
      to help you verify whether sound output and channel balance have returned to normal after cleaning.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it can help and its physical limits</h2>
  <p>
    When a smartphone is exposed to moisture, water drops often do not penetrate into the phone body; instead, they become
    trapped across the exterior speaker grille. Surface tension causes tiny water droplets to cling tightly to the mesh
    pores, blocking the free passage of sound and causing calls, ringtones, and music to sound muffled or distorted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Low-frequency sound waves (such as 165&nbsp;Hz) require larger physical cone excursion than high treble tones. As the
    speaker diaphragm pumps back and forth at full volume, it pushes and pulls air through the acoustic chamber. This
    rapid change in air pressure can overcome the surface tension holding the droplets, dislodging them from the grille so
    they can drop away.
  </p>

  <h3>Hardware limits: what the tool cannot fix</h3>
  <p>
    Acoustic vibration is a surface maintenance technique. It only moves air at the speaker chamber and grille. It cannot:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Repair internal short circuits on the logic board or electronic components.</li>
    <li>Remove water that has reached inside the battery compartment or display assembly.</li>
    <li>Fix speaker cones that are physically torn, punctured, or permanently corroded from prolonged immersion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When to stop and seek repair service</h3>
  <p>Stop using the tool and consult an authorized service provider immediately if you observe any of the following:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The phone will not turn on, restarts repeatedly, or becomes abnormally hot to the touch.</li>
    <li>You notice battery swelling, a bulging back panel, or foggy condensation under the camera lens or display glass.</li>
    <li>A "Liquid Detected" warning persists in the charging port after a full 24 hours of drying.</li>
    <li>The speaker produces no sound at all, or remains severely crackled after multiple cleaning cycles and drying.</li>
    <li>The device was dropped in salt water, chlorinated pool water, or drinks with sugar or acid, which cause corrosion unless professionally cleaned.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How official manufacturer features compare</h2>
  <p>Acoustic water ejection and liquid handling are recognized by major device manufacturers in different ways:</p>

  <h3>Apple Watch Water Lock</h3>
  <p>
    Starting with Apple Watch Series 2, Apple introduced an official feature called <strong>Water Lock</strong>. When you
    finish an activity in water, you turn the Digital Crown to unlock the screen. The watch then emits a sequence of
    low-frequency acoustic tones to eject water from the speaker grille. Apple explains this process in their official
    support guide on <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108351" rel="noopener" target="_blank">how to use Water Lock and eject water on Apple Watch</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    On iPhone, Apple does not include an automatic acoustic ejection button in iOS. Instead, Apple's official advice for
    a wet phone is to gently tap the iPhone against your hand with the connector facing down to remove excess liquid, and
    leave it in a dry, ventilated area. You can read Apple's guidance on <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102643" rel="noopener" target="_blank">what to do if you see a liquid-detection alert on iPhone</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Samsung "Moisture Detected" guidance</h3>
  <p>
    Water-resistant Samsung Galaxy phones feature hardware sensors that check the USB charging port. If moisture is detected,
    One UI displays a water drop icon and disables wired charging to prevent corrosion of the charging pins. Samsung explains
    that water in the charging port typically evaporates naturally within 1 to 2 hours in a well-ventilated space.
  </p>
  <p>
    Samsung also notes that water in the speaker grille can cause quiet or muffled sound until the speaker has dried or
    cleared. Samsung recommends gently shaking the phone with the speaker and port pointing downward, wiping the device with
    a clean, soft cloth, and allowing it to air-dry. See Samsung's official support article on <a href="https://www.samsung.com/uk/support/mobile-devices/what-to-do-if-your-water-resistant-phone-or-tablet-detects-moisture-in-the-charging-port/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">what to do if your Galaxy phone detects moisture</a> and their guide on <a href="https://www.samsung.com/africa_en/support/mobile-devices/there-is-noise-in-the-speaker-sound-of-the-device/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">noise in the speaker sound after liquid contact</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Water-resistance ratings: IP67 vs. IP68 explained</h3>
  <p>
    Water and dust resistance ratings are governed by the international standard <strong>IEC 60529</strong> published by
    the International Electrotechnical Commission. An Ingress Protection (IP) code consists of two digits:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>First digit (solids):</strong> A rating of <strong>6</strong> means "dust tight" — full protection against
      the ingress of dust particles over standard test durations.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Second digit (liquids):</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>7 (IP67):</strong> Protection against the effects of temporary immersion in fresh water up to a depth of
          <strong>1&nbsp;meter for up to 30&nbsp;minutes</strong> under static test conditions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>8 (IP68):</strong> Protection against continuous immersion in fresh water deeper than 1 meter under
          conditions specified by the manufacturer (for instance, Apple tests recent flagship iPhones up to 6 meters for
          30 minutes, while Samsung tests Galaxy flagships up to 1.5 meters for 30 minutes).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It is important to understand that IP tests are conducted in <em>still fresh water</em> in controlled laboratory
    environments. They do not certify protection against high-pressure water jets, sea water, hot shower steam, or chlorinated
    pools. Furthermore, as documented by Apple on their <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108039" rel="noopener" target="_blank">splash, water, and dust resistance page</a>, water resistance is not permanent and decreases naturally over time due to wear, drops, and aging adhesives. Liquid damage is not covered under standard manufacturer warranties.
  </p>

  <h2>Safety and best practices</h2>
  <p>Follow these safety guidelines when using the acoustic tool:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Volume:</strong> Set your device media volume to 100% so the speaker cone has maximum displacement to push air.
      <strong>Do not hold the speaker directly against your ear</strong> while the tone is playing, as continuous loud tones
      can cause hearing discomfort.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Duration:</strong> Run the 60-second cycle once or twice. If moisture remains, wait a few minutes before
      running it again. Running tones non-stop for hours will not dry the device faster and will drain the battery.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Orientation:</strong> Hold the phone so the speaker grille faces downward over a dry cloth or paper towel.
      This ensures dislodged droplets fall out rather than settling back into the mesh.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not use heat:</strong> Never use hair dryers, heat guns, ovens, or radiators. External heat damages the
      lithium-ion battery and softens the waterproof glue seals around the screen and rear glass.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not use compressed air:</strong> High-pressure air can rupture delicate speaker membranes and microphone
      diaphragms.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not use rice:</strong> Putting a wet phone in a bag of uncooked rice is ineffective and can deposit fine
      starch powder inside openings. Apple explicitly warns against using rice. For detailed testing and explanation, read our
      guide on <a href="/guides/does-rice-fix-a-wet-phone">whether rice fixes a wet phone</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not insert sharp objects:</strong> Never poke toothpicks, pins, or cotton swab tips into the speaker grille
      mesh or charging port.
    </li>
  </ul>

